title: 4. Why counted signs set the tier, and who counts
pid: f73e6a70-70f3-47e4-b6ae-388b1b973915
body: |2-
  Every judgement here is a count, so judging costs no mission and a
  minute of reading. The judges are Top for the request, the planner
  for the plan, and Tay for the count: no new specialist and no judging
  mission. Top reads the plan's first lines, not the plan, and briefs
  the readers the tier names. Tay reads every plan, so Tay checks the
  count; lowering the tier takes Top's word.
  First use: Vic counted three one-way doors, so Tay, Libby and Jeff
  read it, and Jeff was asked one question.
  Rests on: read (ITIL's change enablement: approval set by risk,
  standard changes pre-approved; Bezos 2015: the heavy process for
  everything makes an organization slow; Anthropic's research system:
  agents misjudge the effort a task deserves, so the rules are written
  out; Rust's list of what is "substantial"). Ours: the signs, the
  tiers, who counts. Unsure: whether a planner counts its own plan honestly.
